00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.390
Am I using the right platform?

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.815
Am I limited by the
platform I'm using?

00:04:37.815 --> 00:04:40.010
Am I restricting
my possibilities

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.660
with a platform I chose?

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.740
So choosing the provider
of your IoT platform

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.230
is actually really
important, and you often

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:52.130
time forget about the delays of
managing complex infrastructure

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:55.090
when you put IoT
platform in place.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.960
The specialized
tech and learning

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.710
curve you have to have for
hardware communication, AI,

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.600
data ingestion, all
of that, there's

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.000
no real standard protocols.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.790
So it's pretty messy.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:10.520
There's a lot of hidden costs
that come with IoT deployment.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.970
So having a platform that's
simple, flexible, and can

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.430
really help you to
get to value faster

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:20.060
is critical for the success.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.790
We see IoT from
Google's [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:25.750
But we see IoT as a big
data challenge almost more

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.570
than anything else, and
big data in real-time.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.750
And having that ability to gain
real-time insight, as I said,

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.580
will bring the
competitiveness that you're

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:34.705
looking for your business.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.080
So in terms of platform, the
Google Cloud IoT platform

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:45.050
is a serverless platform from
connectivity, processing,

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.480
storage, and analyze.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.070
Serverless means
that you never have

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.740
to worry about
scaling up or down

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.370
or pre-provisioning resources
and overusing resources.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.240
It's just going to scale
from one or a few device

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.640
to millions of
devices very easily.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.240
And that's really a core
differentiation of our platform

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.550
from connectivity to analysis.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:10.420
If we look a little bit deeper
into the Cloud IoT platform

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:14.260
itself, you're going to see
several components here.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:15.880
The main one is Cloud IoT Core.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.730
That's something we launched
GA last year in March,

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:21.130
so about a year ago.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:22.380
That's been pretty successful.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.050
This is a global front end for
your devices, one URL globally.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.810
And we use all the
Google front end--

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.880
the GFE, we call
them-- to ingest data

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.080
from anywhere in the world.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.120
You don't really
have to think about,

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:37.210
where is my device physically
and which region does

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:39.280
it have to connect to to
have the lowest latency.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.110
That will happen automatically.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:43.720
This is served by the same
front end that serve your Google

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.510
search or your YouTube.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.510
It's the same front end.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:50.590
So it's really global, really
scalable all across the globe.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:54.082
Once the data flows into IoT
Core, it flows into Pub/Sub.

00:06:54.082 --> 00:06:57.650
Pub/Sub is this messaging
bus, global again.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.400
Also realize, you don't have
to worry about resources here.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.870
Pub/Sub is available globally.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:05.560
That means that if you have data
ingested in Australia, in Asia,

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.650
in North America, they all
fall into a topic of choice.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.650
You want to get that data,
just query that topic

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.080
and get the data out.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.580
You don't have to think about,
oh, where is my device again.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:19.622
It's all global infrastructure,
automatically scalable.

00:07:19.622 --> 00:07:22.050
Pub/Sub stores data
for seven days.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:26.535
You can do snapshot replays and
have several subscribers to it

00:07:26.535 --> 00:07:27.410
and get the data out.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.410
The data can go into
a Cloud function,

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:33.290
a serverless compute
service to trigger actions,

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:35.090
or do any kind of requests.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:36.890
Or it can go to
Cloud Data Fusion.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.250
Our Cloud Data Fusion is
the managed version of CDAP.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:45.740
CDAP is an open source
data processing pipeline.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:47.870
You may know it as Cask.io.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:50.840
So that's open source, and
you can create your workflow

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:54.730
with drag and drop little nodes,
and then do your transformation

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:55.940
along the way.

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:58.430
Plug the data out of Pub/Sub,
do your transformation,

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.260
and drop it in some database.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.190
If you want to do
longer processes

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:06.080
like windowing
analysis in real-time,

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.730
you can use Dataflow.

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:09.920
Dataflow is also
scalable on its own

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.650
and has an Apache Beam
open source equipment.

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:16.550
All those are managed but have
their open source equivalents.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:17.355
Pretty cool.

00:08:17.355 --> 00:08:19.980
And then you'll land in Bigtable
if you want to do time series.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.930
If you want to do
big data analytics,

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.060
you have your BigQuery.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:25.520
You have Cloud Machine
Learning to do your training.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.070
Send those models back, and
send them back to the device

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:30.330
because Cloud IoT Core
is bi-directional.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.330
And then you have some
nice visualization tools.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:35.210
So that's what we call
the Cloud IoT platform.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.850
Putting that together
is fairly simple.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.960
Doesn't require much
code, if any code.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:41.240
It's all serverless.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.020
And we're pretty happy with
some of the early customers

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:46.010
that we've had.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.810
You can see some
of those customer

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.240
