00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:51.350
But let's step back for a second
to say, why is edge actually

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:51.980
important.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:52.610
Right?

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:54.780
So in a typical
industrial environment,

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.663
whether it's in oil and gas,
manufacturing, transportation,

00:11:57.663 --> 00:11:59.330
smart cities, buildings,
doesn't matter.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:02.300
All of these different sectors,
you've got data coming in--

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.310
I might add-- lots
of data, especially

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:08.350
if you're doing video, audio,
and acoustic type of data.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:13.190
And there is a high cost latency
and security associated with it

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:16.250
by directly connecting these
machines into some kind

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:19.340
of a central or cloud location.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:22.370
What FogHorn really does
is to help with that,

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:27.170
solve the problem, by
introducing this edge device

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:30.440
next to the asset-- on many
cases on the asset itself--

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:34.080
where the data is ingested
into our software running here.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:37.130
And then you derive the insights
and then send the results

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:38.810
and insights back
into the cloud,

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.170
wherefore additional
processing for fine tuning

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:45.350
your models, for fleet-wide
visibility, and so on.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:48.620
This obviously has the
benefits of low latency

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.860
here because there is no latency
involved between these two.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.560
And then there is low
cost of data transport.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.240
And then security is also
eliminated or minimized

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.337
when you're
communicating with that.

00:13:02.337 --> 00:13:04.420
Another way to look at it,
a lot of the times when

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:05.920
you're processing data--

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:10.030
in this example right here,
this is a sensor signal for one

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:10.840
of the signals.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:13.190
You see here how the
blips are in the signal?

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:14.500
This is a suction pump.

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.270
When you typically
send that information

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:20.320
to a down sampled environment,
whether it's cloud data center,

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:21.918
this signal actually
looks like that.

00:13:21.918 --> 00:13:24.460
You actually pretty much miss
the little point of what it is.

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:29.050
So the fidelity of deriving
actionable insights at the edge

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:32.110
is a lot higher, is really why
it's so important, in addition

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:33.580
to all of the
three things that I

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:38.240
talked about-- security, cost,
and, of course, latencies.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:40.100
This is a high level
product solution,

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:42.322
which is our traditional
solution that we've

00:13:42.322 --> 00:13:43.280
been shipping on Linux.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:46.120
It's the same solution that I
showed you on the first slide.

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.530
It has been put onto Android.

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:51.100
The idea here is being
that you ingested data

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.570
from all these different
types of sources,

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:56.380
enrich them because a lot of the
times in the industrial world,

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:58.450
the data quantity is not good.

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:00.970
That layer fixes all of
that, cleanses, normalizes,

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:04.330
decodes it, and then does
processing through these two

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:05.662
layers that I talked about.

00:14:05.662 --> 00:14:07.870
And then the information
itself can then be published

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:08.960
to a cloud environment.

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:11.620
We have pretty deep
integration with IoT Core,

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.690
as Antony mentioned,
and I'll mentioned

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:17.170
that in a second in the next
slide how that is done as well.

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:21.730
And using the SDK, you can then
program and programmatically

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:26.330
subscribe to those insights
and take closed loop actions.

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:28.700
So one concept that I want
to bring into this picture

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.370
is this notion of what
is called edgification.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:32.370
I know it's a new word.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:33.680
So what is edgification?

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:36.020
A lot of the times when
you build data science

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:41.030
models, machine learning models,
to run in a server environment,

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:43.610
cloud environment,
you may or may not

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:46.730
necessarily pay attention to
the amount of compute resources.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:49.670
Or you may be actually
working on batch data

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:51.620
rather than working
on live data.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:53.180
And you may also
not pay attention

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:55.520
to the number of layers
on the size of the model

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:57.230
because those are
not necessarily

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:59.910
the constraints you worry
about in a cloud environment.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:02.480
But when you bring the same
model to run on an edge

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.230
environment, in order to
run efficiently and also

00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:08.252
perform at the rate
that you would expect,

00:15:08.252 --> 00:15:09.710
you have to deal
with those things.

00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:11.960
And that process is what
we call edgification.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.900
How do you take your model
that typically works off

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:18.110
of batch data and make it
run on real-time live data?

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:20.000
How do you then
optimize and reduce

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.130
the number of
layers and the rates

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.750
and still with the
same level of accuracy?

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:27.380
And finally, if we look at the
anatomy of a machine learning

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:30.240
model, it has
really three things.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.952
One is what we call a
pre-processing code, which

00:15:32.952 --> 00:15:34.160
is really feature extraction.

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:35.618
Let's say you're
looking at a video

00:15:35.618 --> 00:15:38.620
or you're looking at some sensor
signals from the machines.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:41.120
Before you can apply a machine
learning model and algorithm,

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:42.410
you've got to extract
some features.

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:44.460
And that's typically what
we call pre-processing,

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:47.450
and that is the most
compute-intensive part of it.

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:49.602
And then followed by you've
passed those features

00:15:49.602 --> 00:15:51.810
into your machine learning
algorithm or the equation,

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:55.160
which itself is not really
that computationally intensive.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.350
And then followed
by post-processing,

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.770
which is how do you then take
the results of that equation

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:01.250
and do something with it.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:03.710
What we do as part of
this edgification process

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:06.550
is to extract all of
the pre-processing,

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:10.400
the post-processing, into our
highly efficient complex event

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.010
processing engine that we
built from ground up, which

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:16.100
runs in really, really small
footprint, few megabytes

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:18.020
of memory, runs really fast.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.540
And then lead the machine
learning part [INAUDIBLE] to it

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:21.798
into your engine.

00:16:21.798 --> 00:16:23.840
Of course, you also have
the ability to, in fact,

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:26.630
do the machine learning part
in the CEP engine itself

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:28.200
as we move forward.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:31.820
Here are some examples where
we took a video-based layer

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.160
modeling use case,
how you can actually

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.290
optimize when you build.

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:38.770
Take a model that is
built here, edgify it.

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:41.690
You got almost a 10x
or higher improvement,

00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:45.515
and yet, much higher fertility,
much higher accurate results.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:52.350
Now, even when you build
a machine learning model

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.428
to derive some insights
and predict some things

00:16:54.428 --> 00:16:56.220
and you deploy this in
the edge environment

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:58.470
or any environment
for that matter,

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:00.570
and it's accurately
predicting the results,

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:02.640
it does not
necessarily mean over

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:04.560
time it's going to
continue to predict

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.420
with the same level of accuracy.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:10.500
Because of data drifts, because
of changes in machine behavior,

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:14.099
the same model that once used to
accurately predict the results

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:16.589
may no longer do that.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:17.730
So how do we address that?

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:18.313
How do we fix?

00:17:18.313 --> 00:17:21.720
That to address that, we came
up with this notion of automated

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:23.290
closed loop machine learning.

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:24.849
So what does that really mean?

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:27.010
So as you see here
in our architecture,

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:28.950
once a model is
deployed, we built

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:33.450
this thing called a prediction
model degradation detector.

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:36.750
What that module does is
that it continuously measures

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:38.020
several different factors--

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:39.750
whether it's an F1
score, whether it's

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:42.150
a data distribution
collection of how things are--

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:45.090
and tries to see if the accuracy
of the model that you're

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:48.090
predicting is deviating
from what it was before.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.670
And when it does, what
it does it automatically

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.580
sends that information to
the Google IoT Core here.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.890
From Google IoT Core,
as Antony explained,

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.360
it's a Pub/Sub system.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.390
You can send that information
to Dataflow, which can then

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:04.320
be read and configured into
BigQuery in the Cloud ML engine

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:07.230
where you retrain
incrementally your model

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.510
with this incremental
data that's coming in.

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:14.280
And then push that model
back onto the edge as well.

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:15.390
This is all automated.

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:17.970
Of course, you have an
option to not automate it.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:19.980
But this is done
in an automated way

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.875
until the accuracy comes
back to what it was before.

00:18:23.875 --> 00:18:26.250
And this is what we have been
calling the closed loop ML.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:28.290
I know different
terminology have been used.

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:32.340
This is truly revolutionizing
the edge computing

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.860
because you're actually
introducing the notion of AI,

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:39.210
artificial intelligence, onto
these edge devices on how

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:41.160
do you self-correct
the models deployed

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:42.840
onto this environment
in order for you

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:48.340
to continue to predict this
with the same level of accuracy.

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:51.490
As you can see, our solution
is a genetic platform.

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:54.130
We have been deploying
this across many, many use

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:56.320
cases across many
different verticals.

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:58.720
Our top verticals
actually include

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:00.910
manufacturing-- a lot
of big name customers--

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:03.965
oil and gas, transportation,
smart cities, buildings.

00:19:03.965 --> 00:19:06.340
We have done other things as
well like mining, and so on.

00:19:06.340 --> 00:19:09.340
What I want to do is
just mention a couple

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:12.880
of use cases quickly here,
one in the manufacturing,

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:14.620
one in oil and
gas, that actually

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:16.840
involves different paradigms.

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:18.910
This is a fact that I
can publicly reference.

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:20.630
It's a GE factory.

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.440
They manufacture these
industrial gated capacitors

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:24.350
that look like this.

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:27.070
This is a highly
expensive capacitor

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:28.390
that are used in power plants.

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.712
The material cost alone is
several thousand dollars.

00:19:30.712 --> 00:19:32.170
And the way the
processing includes

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:34.272
was, they take an aluminum
foil, they wind it

00:19:34.272 --> 00:19:36.730
through what is called a winding
machine called [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:37.893
winding machine.

00:19:37.893 --> 00:19:39.310
And the winding
machine itself has

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:41.350
hundreds of sensors
that are continuously

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:44.110
measuring several things,
winder tension, width aspect

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:45.560
ration, and so on.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:48.760
Once they wind it, they press it
into packs and then insert it.

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:50.020
These are those packs.

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:52.645
And they run it through what is
called a treat oven to take out

00:19:52.645 --> 00:19:53.690
all the moisture content.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:56.200
And finally, they'll fill
oil into the capacitor

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:58.060
to test the capacitor.

00:19:58.060 --> 00:20:00.400
At this point, if
the capacitor fails,

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:03.240
so it's not working or
seeing dielectric failures,

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:04.462
it's too late in the process.

00:20:04.462 --> 00:20:06.670
And that's exactly what was
happening in the factory.

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:08.950
As I walked into the
factory, I see a lot of pink

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:12.160
slips on the factory floor,
costing them a lot of scrap,

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:13.520
millions of dollars.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:15.280
So the challenge
posed for us was,

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.980
how do we connect our edge
solution to the sensors

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:20.020
directly into this assembly
line in the machine,

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:23.320
connect it and identify
exactly what was causing

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.800
these dielectric failures,
more importantly, before it

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:29.920
is too late so that the operator
actually has a chance to go

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:32.080
take the units offline
and then fix it.

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:34.247
And that's what we have
been able to successfully do

00:20:34.247 --> 00:20:38.170
that and reduce all of their
pain points on the scrap.

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:40.850
The second use case I want
to talk about is interesting.

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:43.210
This is an oil and gas use case.

00:20:43.210 --> 00:20:44.920
Saudi Aramco is one
of our customers.

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:46.330
Many other customers as well.

00:20:46.330 --> 00:20:49.570
We've actually publicly talked
about this at the RC conference

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:51.080
a couple of months ago.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:53.290
This is, as some of you
may be familiar with,

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:55.900
in oil and gas refineries,
gas is being refined.

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:57.760
You've got hundreds
of compressors.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:01.090
And because of various
reasons, excessive pressure,

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:03.850
sometimes other reasons,
compressor failures, the gas

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.460
gets released in through
what are called oil stacks.

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:08.050
These are all oil stacks.

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:09.610
And then the gas gets burned.

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:11.710
When gas gets burned,
you see a fire.

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:14.740
What's worse, you can
see smoke sometimes.

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:15.790
Fire itself is bad.

00:21:15.790 --> 00:21:16.900
Smoke is even worse.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:18.610
Dark smoke is even worse.

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:20.200
So how to identify?

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:22.180
Up until before this
edge solution came up,

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:24.550
typically, they
installed video cameras.

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:26.020
Sometimes they
don't even do that.

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:28.970
And a human being is able
to monitor it 24 by 7,

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:30.220
which is not really practical.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:32.095
All they can do is to
see, oh, there is fire.

00:21:32.095 --> 00:21:32.800
There is smoke.

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:35.290
What we have done is
installed our software

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:38.440
on a controller box attached
to these oil stacks--

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:39.910
many, many, many of them--

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:43.000
and then directly take
the video feed from this,

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:45.850
combine it with the audio
feed of the compressor,

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:47.860
and identify and
correlate one, there

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:51.470
is in fact a flare
or a smoke and also

00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:54.110
compute the volumetric flow
of the gas that is burned,

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:55.340
and things of that nature.

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:58.300
So human being and monitoring
of this is eliminated.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:00.880
But more importantly,
operator gets an alert

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:03.513
when there is a real problem,
and we get to the root cause

00:22:03.513 --> 00:22:04.930
and why the problem
was occurring.

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:09.100
This is being widely deployed
in a very popular use case.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:12.480
One last thing I want to say is
that this particular session,

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:14.150
the rest of the
talk and the demo,

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:16.150
remember as we talked
about, is our product that

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:21.130
was released on the handheld
devices, mobile edge platforms.

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:23.050
We have noticed that
there is obviously

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.840
a lot of traction in
this field because these

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.450
are all battery-powered devices,
which makes it even more

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:32.290
important for our edge computing
to consume less and less power,

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:34.000
less and less CPU and memory.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:36.550
And we imported our
software onto these devices.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:40.240
And deploying with Honeywell,
especially initially working

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:41.770
with Honeywell
device where we have

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:44.145
done a couple of use cases,
which we'll demonstrate today

00:22:44.145 --> 00:22:44.660
as well.

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:47.057
With that, I'd like to invite
Scott Bracken, Director

00:22:47.057 --> 00:22:48.640
of Advanced Technology
from Honeywell,

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:50.940
to speak about their technology.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:54.310
SCOTT BRACKEN: Great, thank you.

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:56.300
Thank you, Sastry.

00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:57.120
Hello, everyone.

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.090
My name is Scott Bracken,
as Sastry mentioned,

00:22:59.090 --> 00:23:00.800
and I lead the
Advanced Technology

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:05.600
