customer's application.

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:46.860
But on the edge of
that application

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:49.380
and during different use
cases, many of the images

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:52.260
we receive from our
device on our imager come

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.430
in very blurry or very noisy.

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:56.100
It's very common.

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:58.170
New operators that
haven't operated a scanner

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:03.240
before don't necessarily
aim the device accurately.

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:05.370
Or I'm sure many of you
have been at a retail store

00:30:05.370 --> 00:30:07.770
when a particular
packaging, maybe

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:10.710
a shrink wrapped packaging
that has some ripple in it,

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:14.460
is causing the scan to
be difficult to read

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:15.500
for that device.

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:18.590
And an operator,
naturally many times,

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:22.010
wants to go closer to that
barcode to pick up that scan.

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:25.880
And in fact, they're making it
more blurry by going closer.

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:28.850
So again, there's several
different environments

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:33.980
where a blurry barcode or being
able to decode a blurry barcode

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:37.510
would be much more efficient
for our customer base.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:40.110
And what you see
here is a data set.

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:45.070
So we created a synthetic data
set of 50,000 barcode images.

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:47.480
They are image
pairs, clean images

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:50.120
and blurry or damaged images.

00:30:50.120 --> 00:30:53.590
And we use that for training
our machine learning model

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:55.700
that then we deploy
through the FogHorn system

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:57.680
onto our Mobility Edge device.

00:30:57.680 --> 00:31:00.320
And these are just some
snippets of the images that were

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:02.210
part of our training data set.

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:06.440
So that's our first use
case, and our second one

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:10.170
is quite basic but very
important to our customers.

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:12.000
So these devices are
owned by the customer.

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:13.417
They're not owned
by the employee.

00:31:13.417 --> 00:31:15.060
They're not their
personal devices.

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:18.290
So the typical method
for these being deployed

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:20.300
is at the beginning of
a shift, the operator

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:22.430
will take the device, use
it throughout the shift,

00:31:22.430 --> 00:31:25.160
and then dock it for
charging overnight.

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:27.050
The very simple
requirement is this

00:31:27.050 --> 00:31:29.360
that device must
operate continuously

00:31:29.360 --> 00:31:31.100
throughout that entire shift.

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:32.540
That shift can vary in time.

00:31:32.540 --> 00:31:37.950
It can vary in operation and
what modes are being used.

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:42.350
And so by applying the machine
learning type of approach

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:45.410
to this and working with the
data scientists at FogHorn,

00:31:45.410 --> 00:31:50.210
we're able to create a real-time
operating model on the Mobility

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:53.000
Edge device that can
monitor and optimize

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:56.360
to extend the life of that
battery for that device

00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:57.950
during the shift.

